+---
+name: hdae-development
+description: Apply Human-Directed Agentic Engineering through proportional planning, agent-executed loops, and human-approved gates when durable control is needed. Use for substantial new or existing engineering work that needs clear architecture, milestones, evidence, and stopping points.
+---
+
+# HDAE Development
+
+Human-Directed Agentic Engineering (HDAE) combines informed human direction
+with bounded agent autonomy. The human owns product intent, domain decisions,
+architecture, constraints, priorities, and review boundaries. The agent turns
+that direction into artifacts and code, runs repeatable implementation and
+diagnosis loops, and presents evidence at agreed boundaries.
+
+HDAE is not continual approval of individual edits, and it is not unguided
+"vibe coding." Give the agent room to iterate inside an approved request or
+gate while returning architectural and product decisions to the human.
+
+Read repository instructions and inspect the current project before proposing
+new structure. Preserve useful existing plans, tests, terminology, and decision
+records rather than creating parallel documents.
+
+## Select the operation
+
+- **Establish:** turn an idea into product intent, architecture, constraints,
+  milestones, and executable gates.
+- **Implement:** execute the approved gate or ordered gate batch.
+- **Verify:** independently determine whether a gate passed.
+- **Maintain:** handle bounded defects directly or convert larger defects,
+  requests, and operational evidence into gates without losing the original
+  architecture.
+- **Retrospective:** examine how direction, evidence, and iteration affected the
+  outcome and improve the process.
+
+If the operation or need for a gate is unclear, establish it with the human
+before making substantial changes.
+
+## Discovery interview
+
+Start Establish mode by interviewing the human. Do not require the human to
+arrive with a completed brief or fill out a template.
+
+1. Ask a small first round of the highest-value questions about the problem,
+   intended outcome, users, and why it matters.
+2. Use each answer to ask focused follow-ups about architecture, constraints,
+   non-goals, risks, existing behavior, and proof of success.
+3. Inspect the repository between question rounds so questions reflect the
+   actual system rather than generic assumptions.
+4. Continue until the answers and repository evidence are sufficient to propose
+   a coherent project brief and gate map. Do not stop questioning merely because
+   every topic has been mentioned once.
+5. Summarize the resulting understanding in plain language and ask the human to
+   correct it before treating it as authoritative.
+
+Prefer one to three related questions at a time. Explain why a question changes
+the architecture, scope, or acceptance criteria when that is not obvious.
+
+## Discuss the plan before work
+
+Before doing task work, discuss the plan with the human. At minimum, state the
+intended outcome, work to perform, scope and non-goals, acceptance evidence, and
+stopping point. This discussion always occurs; scale its detail to the work. A
+small task may need only a short summary, while a long project needs explicit
+goals, an ordered plan, dependencies, gates, and a task list with current
+statuses.
+
+For work expected to take a long time, tell the human when the autonomous inner
+loop will begin, what it will cover, and when progress checkpoints will occur.
+Give an ETA range and confidence when evidence supports one. If a useful ETA is
+not yet possible, say why and identify the checkpoint at which it can be
+reassessed. Update the plan and estimate when evidence changes; do not present
+speculation as a commitment.
+
+## Decide whether a gate is needed
+
+Not every request requires a gate. Do not create a gate for a small, bounded
+request that is reasonably expected to finish in one prompt or one near-single
+implementation-and-verification loop without surviving a context compaction,
+handoff, or later checkpoint. Discuss the concise plan, perform the work,
+verify it proportionally, and report the result without adding gate-ledger
+ceremony.
+
+Use a gate when the work is expected to need multiple implementation loops or
+human checkpoints, has a meaningful chance of spanning context compaction or
+handoff, or needs durable tracking of scope, decisions, tasks, and evidence.
+When the boundary is uncertain, ask the human whether to use an HDAE gate for
+iteration and review before creating one. Once a gate is chosen, complete gate
+alignment and review it with the human as required below.
+
+## Ask the user when direction is unclear
+
+The user supplies authoritative guidance. Whenever a goal, gate boundary,
+scope, priority, acceptance criterion, expected result, or interpretation of
+observed results is unclear, incomplete, conflicting, or materially ambiguous,
+ask the user a focused question instead of assuming an answer. State what is
+known, what remains unclear, and why the answer affects the work. Wait for the
+user before continuing any affected implementation, verification, acceptance,
+or gate transition; only unrelated work that cannot prejudge the answer may
+continue.
+
+When user, automation, skill, repository, or global agent instructions conflict,
+identify the conflicting sources and explain their practical effect instead of
+silently choosing one or merely stopping. Continue to obey the prevailing
+higher-priority instruction, and ask the user how they want the mutable
+lower-priority instruction resolved. When the user authorizes the correction
+and it is in scope, update the source instruction yourself and then reload the
+applicable instructions before resuming. If you cannot change it, give the user
+the exact file, setting, or policy location and the smallest clear change or
+command needed. State plainly when a system, developer, safety, permission, or
+other higher-priority constraint cannot be overridden by user guidance.
+
+## Converge without spinning
+
+Continue the autonomous inner loop while the approved request or gate is
+incomplete, the next action remains in scope, and each cycle has a credible
+chance of producing measurable progress or new evidence. Never repeat an
+unchanged attempt. Every retry must change a hypothesis, input, implementation,
+environment, or diagnostic and explain why that change could help.
+
+When an approach stalls, preserve what was tried and choose a materially
+different in-scope action with the highest expected information value. Stop and
+return to the human when a required decision, credential, permission, or
+dependency is missing; remaining alternatives would expand scope or risk;
+repeated cycles produce no new evidence or progress; expected time or token cost
+is disproportionate to the gate; or no credible in-scope alternative remains.
+Report the attempts, evidence, alternatives, and recommendation, and persist the
+checkpoint. Difficulty alone is not a blocker; exhaust sensible bounded
+alternatives first.
+
+## Turn discovered defects into proposed gates
+
+When execution exposes a credible defect outside the approved gate, do not
+silently fix it or stop at a vague scope question. Preserve the reproduction,
+impact, and blocking relationship in the active checkpoint, then draft the
+smallest corrective gate when the defect blocks acceptance, needs multiple
+loops, or requires durable verification. Show its proposed position and
+dependencies, goal, scope and non-goals, deterministic acceptance evidence,
+delivery strategy, and review policy. Recommend whether the active gate should
+remain blocked, be deferred, or resume after the corrective gate.
+
+Prefer a new gate so the original approval and evidence remain intact. Expand
+an existing open gate only when the human explicitly chooses that option after
+seeing the scope and verification changes; never modify a closed gate. Ask the
+human whether to create and execute the proposed corrective gate. Prior approval
+of the active gate or batch does not authorize the new work. After approval,
+create the gate, complete any remaining alignment, and execute it through the
+normal HDAE loop instead of waiting for the human to restate the bug.
+
+## Recover context before continuing
+
+At task start and after any compaction, context reset, resume, or handoff,
+reload the complete active copy of this `SKILL.md` before task work continues.
+Then reload repository instructions, any reference this skill requires for the
+active operation, and the authoritative HDAE project checkpoint. Compacted chat
+or a remembered summary is not a substitute for those sources.
+
+Confirm the active gate, scope, status, evidence, open decisions, and exact next
+action against the checkpoint before resuming the inner loop. Every long-running
+checkpoint must identify the active skill, its project discovery path, and that
+reload is required after a context shift. Read the recovery procedure in
+[references/hdae-method.md](references/hdae-method.md) when establishing or
+resuming durable project work.
+
+## Maintain the filesystem gate ledger
+
+Every approved gate must have one authoritative Markdown file under the
+project's root `hdae/` directory. The directory is lifecycle state:
+`gates-todo/` contains open work, `gates-done/` contains passed gates closed by
+human review or confirmed batch delegation, and `gates-deferred/` contains
+intentionally postponed gates. The leading decimal filename component is
+execution position. Do not duplicate path-derived lifecycle or order in gate
+indexes or metadata fields.
+
+Keep completed work, remaining work, criteria, verification plan, evidence,
+decisions, limitations, and review policy in that gate file. A technically
+passed gate remains in `gates-todo/` until it reaches its confirmed review
+boundary: explicit human acceptance for a checkpoint gate or automatic closure
+for a delegated gate. Every file in `gates-done/` is a locked historical
+record: never edit, rename, reorder, or delete it. If later work is needed,
+create a new gate. Only an explicit human direction may reopen a closed gate;
+move the unchanged file to `gates-todo/` before making any modification.
+Move a gate to `gates-deferred/` only with the reason and return condition
+recorded; move it back to `gates-todo/` when reactivated. Read the gate-ledger
+procedure and shell commands in
+[references/hdae-method.md](references/hdae-method.md) before creating,
+inserting, selecting, deferring, completing, or migrating gates.
+
+## Update an installed HDAE copy
+
+When the human asks to update a copied HDAE installation, run the current
+canonical installer from GitHub `main`; never execute or expect
+`.hdae/install.sh`, and do not add Python. Determine the project root and run:
+
+```sh
+curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gmkurtzer/hdae/main/install.sh |
+    sh -s -- --target "$project_root" --all --copy
+```
+
+The installer must stop on locally modified managed skills or recovery
+instructions. Do not add `--force` without explicit human authorization after
+showing what would be replaced. Every install or update must ensure both
+supported discovery roots, `.agents/skills/` and `.claude/skills/`, link to the
+same `.hdae/skills/` copies; `--codex` and `--claude` are compatibility aliases
+for `--all`, not selective installs.
+
+Afterward, verify every configured discovery link, `.hdae/manifest`, and skill
+contents; confirm `.hdae/install.sh` is absent and the manifest contains no
+installer-copy ownership field. Immediately reload the complete newly installed
+`hdae-development/SKILL.md` through the active agent discovery path and read
+every reference it requires for the current operation before continuing. Do
+this in the current turn when the environment permits it. If the runtime cannot
+reload a changed skill, stop and give the human the exact command or restart
+action needed before claiming the update is active. Review the dependency diff
+and commit only when authorized by repository practice.
+
+## Check HDAE for updates
+
+When the human asks to check HDAE for updates, perform a read-only comparison;
+do not install or replace anything. Confirm the project has `.hdae/skills`,
+clone GitHub `main` into a new temporary directory, compare its `skills/` tree
+with the installed tree using `diff -qr`, report whether changes are available
+and summarize changed paths, then remove the temporary directory. A difference
+may be an upstream update or an uncommitted local modification, so inspect the
+project manifest and repository diff before labeling it. If an update is
+available, offer the update workflow above; never combine checking and updating
+without the human's request.
+
+Use this portable shape, keeping the temporary path explicit and validated:
+
+```sh
+project_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd -P)
+hdae_check_dir=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/hdae-check.XXXXXX") || exit 2
+cleanup_hdae_check() {
+    [ -z "$hdae_check_dir" ] || rm -rf "$hdae_check_dir"
+}
+trap cleanup_hdae_check 0 HUP INT TERM
+git clone --quiet --depth 1 --branch main \
+    https://github.com/gmkurtzer/hdae.git "$hdae_check_dir/source" || exit 2
+diff -qr "$project_root/.hdae/skills" "$hdae_check_dir/source/skills"
+hdae_diff_status=$?
+case "$hdae_diff_status" in
+    0) printf '%s\n' 'HDAE is current with main.' ;;
+    1) printf '%s\n' 'HDAE content differences found.' ;;
+    *) printf '%s\n' 'HDAE update check failed.' >&2; exit "$hdae_diff_status" ;;
+esac
+```
+
+Capture `diff` status `0` as current and status `1` as content differences;
+other statuses mean the check failed. Remove only the validated directory made
+by `mktemp`, including on interruption or a failed comparison.
+
+## Choose gate delivery strategy
+
+When creating each gate, ask whether its work belongs on the current branch or
+on a dedicated branch intended for a pull request. Do not ask again when the
+human has already made an explicit choice that covers that exact gate. Record
+the choice in the gate file before implementation, including the working
+branch, intended base branch when relevant, and whether a pull request is
+intended.
+
+Work originating from an issue or pull-request review is an exception: always
+create a dedicated branch intended for a new pull request, and do not offer the
+current branch as an option. Record the originating issue or pull request in the
+gate and reference it in the new pull request. Name the branch
+`hdae/<descriptive-slug>` unless the human or repository defines another branch
+prefix. Use a closing keyword only when
+the work fully resolves an issue and the human's policy permits closure;
+otherwise use a non-closing reference. A saved automatic-management or
+proactive-remediation policy authorizes pushing the dedicated branch and
+opening or updating its pull request as routine delivery; do not ask again.
+Never push directly to the repository's default branch, including `main`, and
+never close or merge a pull request.
+
+Use one gate, one dedicated branch, and one pull request for each independently
+reviewable issue fix. Do not put unrelated gates or issues on the same branch or
+pull request. Combine related issues into one gate and pull request only when
+they share the same root cause or inseparable change and verification set;
+record that relationship and the reason separate delivery would be misleading
+or unsafe in the gate.
+
+Under an automatic-management or proactive-remediation policy, local commits
+are not delivery and do not complete the gate. Finish the loop by pushing the
+dedicated branch, opening or updating the pull request, recording its stable URL
+in the gate, and notifying the human. Keep the gate in `gates-todo/` and report
+delivery as incomplete if the remote branch or pull request is missing. Retry a
+recoverable delivery failure with a materially different action; stop only for
+a meaningful credential, permission, provider, or policy blocker.
+
+Plan commits as part of the gate. A gate is large when it contains multiple
+independently reviewable changes, components, migrations, or risk boundaries.
+Use multiple coherent commits for such a gate so each commit has one clear
+purpose and is testable where practical. Do not split tightly coupled work into
+artificial commits. Record the planned commits before work and the actual
+commits or material deviations before closure. Follow repository signing and
+commit conventions. Outside an automatic-management or proactive-remediation
+policy, use only an explicit current authorization to push or open a pull
+request.
+
+## Align batches and checkpoints
+
+When the human requests several gates, a range, or all todo gates, inspect them
+in numeric order and propose one execution batch before starting. Show the
+included gates, dependencies, expected work, acceptance evidence, and a
+checkpoint map. Recommend a checkpoint for gates involving unresolved product
+or architecture decisions, subjective user acceptance, security or privacy
+risk, migrations, irreversible or consequential external effects, material
+scope changes, or any boundary the human requested. Recommend automatic
+completion only for objective, reversible, in-scope work with complete criteria
+and no new consequential decision.
+
+Ask the human to confirm both the batch and checkpoint map. That confirmation
+may delegate closure of passed non-checkpoint gates and advancement to the next
+gate in this batch only. It does not authorize future gates, failed criteria,
+scope expansion, consequential external action, or bypassing a required
+permission. If the conversation already confirms the exact batch and map, do
+not ask again. Read the multi-gate procedure in
+[references/hdae-method.md](references/hdae-method.md) before running a batch.
+
+A scheduled `$pr-issue-review` task may instead carry a human-confirmed standing
+delegation for a narrowly defined class of future issue- or PR-originated gates.
+Within that recorded repository, risk, scope, validation, delivery, and review
+policy, create and execute qualifying gates without requesting approval again.
+This is not a general batch authorization and does not extend beyond the saved
+policy.
+
+## Mandatory gate alignment
+
+This section applies only after the work has been determined to require a gate.
+Before implementation, align the gates with the human. Skip this only when the
+current conversation already contains explicit approval of the exact gate or
+batch, its acceptance criteria, and its checkpoint map, or when a scheduled
+`$pr-issue-review` task has an applicable standing delegation as described
+above. Under that delegation, perform and persist the same alignment analysis,
+but do not pause merely to ask the human to approve the derived gate, branch,
+criteria, plan, or checkpoint map.
+
+1. Restate the outcome and important constraints in plain language.
+2. Ask focused questions about missing product behavior, architecture,
+   compatibility, operations, user experience, documentation, tests, and
+   acceptance. Ask only questions that could change scope or a gate.
+3. Inspect the relevant repository and existing evidence before finalizing the
+   plan.
+4. Present a proposed gate map in plain, concrete language. For every gate show
+   its goal, observable outcome, included and excluded work, dependencies,
+   deliverables, acceptance evidence, verification plan, and proposed review
+   policy. For substantial work, also show the ordered plan and task list that
+   implement the gates.
+5. Perform a coverage review across functionality, failure behavior,
+   regression testing, user workflow, documentation, compatibility or
+   migration, and operational or security concerns when relevant. Mark a
+   category not applicable rather than silently omitting it.
+6. Explicitly ask the human: “What is missing or incorrectly scoped, which
+   gates may run, and where should I stop for review?”
+7. Wait for approval. Do not treat silence, general enthusiasm, or approval of
+   the overall idea as approval of a gate batch or checkpoint map.
+
+For a delegated scheduled review, replace steps 6 and 7 with autonomous
+execution unless a meaningful blocker exists. Meaningful blockers include an
+ambiguous product or architecture decision, nonpublic security or privacy risk
+that cannot be handled safely under the saved policy, missing credentials or
+permission, work outside the saved scope or risk policy,
+destructive or irreversible impact, conflicting evidence that changes the
+intended result, or failed verification with no credible safe in-scope path.
+Gate numbering, the required dedicated branch, routine commit planning, and
+deterministic test selection are not blockers. Report and ask one focused
+question only when a blocker actually requires human direction. When safe,
+persist the gate and blocker before stopping so the work is not lost. Do not
+write confidential vulnerability details to a public repository; use a
+sanitized gate reference or stop before writing when even that would disclose
+the issue.
+
+When creating or revising a gate, maximize deterministic verification. Map
+every acceptance criterion to an automated test, command, or reproducible
+procedure and state its expected result in the gate's `Verification plan`.
+Prefer controlled inputs, stable fixtures, machine-checkable outputs, and
+explicit exit status or state assertions. If deterministic verification is not
+practical, record why, name the most objective repeatable evidence available,
+and state whether human judgment or an external dependency is required. Human
+review must not replace a check that can reasonably be automated.
+
+When the human adds or changes a requirement, update the affected gate map and
+acceptance criteria. If the change is material, obtain approval before
+continuing implementation.
+
+If any gate, goal, criterion, expected result, or observed outcome is unclear at
+alignment or later execution, stop the affected work and ask the user for
+guidance. Never silently resolve that ambiguity through an assumption.
+
+## Persist shared understanding
+
+Do not rely on chat history as the project record. During Establish mode,
+identify the repository's existing authoritative documents and propose where
+the HDAE state will live. After the human confirms the summarized understanding,
+write or update durable repository files covering:
+
+- project intent, users, outcomes, scope, and non-goals;
+- architecture, constraints, invariants, and important decisions;
+- milestone and gate map with status, dependencies, and acceptance criteria;
+- for long projects, traceability from goals through gates, criteria, tasks, and
+  evidence, plus a record of approved scope changes;
+- the active checkpoint: current gate, evidence, limitations, open questions,
+  required human decisions, exact next action, active skill and discovery path,
+  and whether skill reload is required after a context shift.
+
+Use existing authoritative product and architecture documents when possible,
+but keep the HDAE control plane under root `hdae/`: `PROJECT.md`,
+`ARCHITECTURE.md`, `PLAN.md`, `STATUS.md`, and the three gate lifecycle
+directories. Keep one source of truth for each fact and link existing project
+documents rather than copying them.
+
+For substantial or long-running work, read
+[references/hdae-method.md](references/hdae-method.md) and use its scalable
+planning, traceability, and completeness-audit guidance. Do not rely on a chat
+plan or transient task UI as the authoritative project record.
+
+At the beginning of every resumed task or after a context shift, read the
+repository instructions and these authoritative HDAE files before proposing or
+executing work. At every gate boundary and before handing off, update the active
+checkpoint so another agent can continue without reconstructing decisions from
+conversation history. If chat and persisted state disagree, surface the conflict
+to the human instead of choosing silently.
+
+When version control is available, include the state update with the related
+gate commit when authorized and consistent with repository practice. Otherwise,
+report the uncommitted state files explicitly; never imply that chat alone is a
+durable handoff.
+
+## Establish the project
+
+1. Run the discovery interview until the desired outcome, users, non-goals,
+   constraints, domain assumptions, and definition of success are clear enough
+   for human confirmation.
+2. Inspect the repository or intended environment and identify existing
+   behavior, architecture, tests, and unresolved risks.
+3. Persist the confirmed understanding and active checkpoint using the smallest
+   useful set of authoritative repository documents.
+4. Establish a reproducible baseline. Use a branch, tag, or checkpoint when it
+   materially improves comparison or recovery and the human authorizes it.
+5. Divide the work into reviewable milestones. Each gate must name deliverables,
+   positive and negative acceptance criteria, regression checks, a
+   criterion-by-criterion verification plan with expected results, evidence,
+   and a stopping point.
+6. Complete the mandatory gate-alignment conversation. Planning alone does not
+   authorize implementation.
+
+Read [references/hdae-method.md](references/hdae-method.md) when designing or
+assessing the project process.
+
+## Implement an approved gate or batch
+
+1. Restate the human-approved gate or batch, checkpoint map, scope, invariants,
+   and acceptance commands, and create or locate each `gates-todo/` record. If
+   any are missing, return to gate alignment.
+2. Reproduce the current behavior or missing capability when practical.
+3. Implement the narrowest coherent change that can satisfy the gate.
+4. Iterate autonomously within that boundary under the convergence rules:
+   build, run, observe, diagnose, revise, and rerun.
+5. Keep the human informed at the promised checkpoints. Update task status,
+   plan, risks, and ETA when actual evidence changes them.
+6. Turn discovered regressions into repeatable tests rather than fixing only
+   the visible symptom.
+7. Preserve failures, limitations, commands, and material decisions as evidence.
+8. Reconcile every deliverable and acceptance criterion against fresh evidence,
+   including documentation and user-facing behavior. For long projects, perform
+   the reference's forward, reverse, coverage, and orphan checks. Do not declare
+   the gate complete because its main code path works.
+9. Reconcile source, generated, vendored, and installed copies when relevant;
+   inspect documentation, repository state, commits, and temporary artifacts.
+10. Update the gate file's completed work, remaining work, evidence, decisions,
+    limitations, review policy and outcome, and exact next action, plus
+    `STATUS.md`.
+11. At a checkpoint gate, present the completion summary and stop for human
+    review. At a delegated gate, move it to `gates-done/` only after it passes,
+    record that it closed under the confirmed batch policy, and continue to the
+    next authorized gate. At batch completion, summarize every gate and stop.
+
+Return immediately for human direction when work would change the architecture,
+expand product scope, weaken an acceptance criterion, require consequential
+external action, or choose among materially different product outcomes.
+
+## Verify and maintain
+
+Verify observable behavior from a clean and relevant state. A gate is
+**passed** only when every mandatory criterion ran and succeeded; otherwise use
+**failed**, **blocked**, **not run**, or **partial** precisely.
+**Accepted** means the human reviewed and accepted a passed checkpoint gate.
+**Closed** means a passed gate was either accepted or completed under a
+human-confirmed batch delegation. Never imply that delegated closure was
+individual human review.
+
+A gate cannot pass until its authoritative file records every criterion and the
+fresh evidence that satisfied it. Technical passage alone leaves the file in
+`gates-todo/`; acceptance or confirmed delegated closure moves it to
+`gates-done/`.
+
+For maintenance, connect each issue or request to the authoritative product and
+architecture documents. When the work meets the gate threshold, add the
+smallest gate that reproduces the problem, protects the intended behavior, and
+records the decision. Handle smaller bounded requests without a gate while
+still verifying the intended behavior. Do not let accumulated patches silently
+redefine the system.
+
+## Retrospective
+
+Identify which outcomes came from human direction, agent proposals, executable
+evidence, or correction after failure. Improve documents, tests, gates, or
+repository instructions only when the evidence shows a recurring need.
