new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1af573e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/performance.md
@@ -0,0 +1,134 @@
+# Reproducible performance measurement
+
+Ginux performance claims are tied to named workloads, checked-in
+configurations, raw samples, and a matched Rocky Linux host. Native execution is
+the 1.00x control. A result above 1.00x is better and a result below 1.00x is
+worse, regardless of whether the underlying metric is throughput or latency.
+
+## Compared execution models
+
+| Report label | Execution | Syscall behavior |
+|---|---|---|
+| Native | Ordinary Rocky Linux | No Ginux instance or mediation; control only |
+| Selective | Ginux native backend | Contract-allowed and independently cataloged direct-safe syscalls may execute directly; all other operations remain Ginux-controlled |
+| Paranoid | Ginux native backend | Every syscall enters the Ginux userspace decision path before completion |
+
+Native is sometimes described as the `none` comparison because it has no Ginux
+mediation. It is not a supported value for `[contract.syscalls].control` and is
+not a Ginux containment mode.
+
+## Fixed protocol
+
+Gate 15 reuses Gate 11's pinned Rocky/EPEL
+`sysbench-1.0.20-19.el10_2.x86_64` executable. The host and instance copies must
+have the same SHA-256 digest. Every selective and paranoid row is paired with
+native samples collected in the same four-vCPU Rocky QEMU host session using:
+
+- the same executable and workload arguments;
+- the same 1 GiB address-space limit;
+- the same logical-CPU affinity and vCPU allocation;
+- one warmup per side and row;
+- alternating native/Ginux order by repetition; and
+- the same host kernel, image, background environment, and audit setting.
+
+The full suite covers CPU, memory, thread-yield, and mutex workloads. It runs
+one thread at 1, 2, and 4 allocated vCPUs, plus allocation-scaled 2-thread and
+4-thread rows. The checked-in protocol uses five-second workloads and five
+repetitions. Mutex uses the pinned sysbench defaults because a shortened loop
+would measure startup rather than sustained mutex behavior.
+
+## Templates
+
+The complete reproducibility inputs live under
+[`benchmarks/gate15/`](../benchmarks/gate15/):
+
+- `suite.conf` selects duration, repetitions, allocations, workloads, audit,
+  and supervisor placement;
+- `native.conf` fixes the control identity;
+- `selective.conf` fixes the selective Ginux identity; and
+- `paranoid.conf` fixes the paranoid Ginux identity.
+- `cpu.conf`, `memory.conf`, `threads.conf`, and `mutex.conf` record every
+  workload's exact direction, unit, thread source, duration source, and fixed
+  sysbench parameters.
+
+Copy all four files to customize a run. The scripts reject unknown modes,
+duplicate or unsupported workload/allocation entries, incomplete profiles, and
+incompatible paired metadata before reporting results.
+
+## Running the matrix
+
+Prepare the pinned executable and image once with networking enabled:
+
+```sh
+make bench-modes-prepare
+```
+
+The preparation cache is retained. Subsequent collection is offline:
+
+```sh
+make bench-modes
+```
+
+For a quick end-to-end check:
+
+```sh
+make demo-gate-15
+```
+
+To use copied templates and a separate output directory:
+
+```sh
+GINUX_GATE15_CONFIG_DIR=/tmp/my-gate15 \
+GINUX_GATE15_OUTPUT_DIR=build/my-gate15 \
+    make bench-modes
+```
+
+Regenerate the default report without rerunning workloads:
+
+```sh
+make bench-modes-report
+```
+
+## Outputs
+
+The default output directory is `build/gate15/`:
+
+| Path | Contents |
+|---|---|
+| `results.tsv` | Combined native/selective/paranoid raw samples with paired-control identity |
+| `report.md` | Human-readable means, sample standard deviations, normalized ratios, semantics, and metadata |
+| `performance.svg` | One native-relative graph row per workload/allocation/thread combination |
+| `config/` | Exact templates copied into the run |
+| `selective/` | Selective pair results, metadata, instance recipe, raw transcripts, and bounded audit evidence |
+| `paranoid/` | Paranoid pair results, metadata, instance recipe, raw transcripts, and bounded audit evidence |
+
+The validator requires the same commit, tree state, duration, repetitions,
+affinity, memory limit, audit setting, host kernel, host resources, executable,
+Rocky release, and OCI image across the selective and paranoid pairs. Dispatch
+mode and normalized instance-configuration digest are expected to differ.
+
+## Interpretation and limits
+
+Each Ginux value is normalized to the native samples collected beside that
+mode, not to a native run from another day. The graph shows performance ratios,
+so a higher bar is always better even for lower-is-better mutex latency. Tables
+retain means, sample standard deviation, units, and both paired native values;
+no aggregate score can conceal an individual row.
+
+Selective and paranoid do not offer identical security behavior. Performance
+must be interpreted alongside their syscall-control semantics. The matrix does
+not establish universal application performance, production capacity,
+multi-instance density, hardware-accelerated behavior, or a new tolerance.
+Gate 11 remains authoritative for native-parity goals and exceptions; Gate 13
+uses its own current-main regression boundary.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- A missing dependency or image means `make bench-modes-prepare` has not
+  completed on the persistent Gate 11 benchmark host.
+- A metadata mismatch means the two pairs are not directly comparable. Rerun
+  the complete matrix rather than editing metadata.
+- A validation failure for missing rows means collection stopped early; retain
+  the mode-specific raw directory for diagnosis and rerun the full matrix.
+- Generated results remain under ignored `build/`; copy them intentionally when
+  sharing evidence, together with all metadata and templates.
diff --git a/docs/security-assurance.md b/docs/security-assurance.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..31d9540
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/security-assurance.md
@@ -0,0 +1,156 @@
+# Ginux defensive security assurance
+
+This document records the security-review boundary established by HDAE Gates
+32 through 37. The review is constructive and defensive: it inspects Ginux's
+own source, runs controlled fixtures, and fixes weaknesses. It does not develop
+exploits, build weaponized payloads, or test third-party systems.
+
+## Trust and authority inventory
+
+| Seam | Trusted owner | Required invariant | Primary evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Operator CLI | `ginux-cli` | Unprivileged parsing cannot directly mutate privileged state | CLI unit tests and helper protocol tests |
+| Privileged lifecycle helper | `ginux-host-instance-helper.sh` | Instance names, paths, deployment state, and transitions are validated before host mutation | instance, lifecycle, and recovery suites |
+| OCI acquisition | `ginux-oci` | Content is digest verified; archive paths and links cannot escape the selected root | OCI layer and cache-integrity tests |
+| Contract compiler | `ginux-contract` and `ginux-instance` | Unknown syntax fails closed; compiled decisions are deterministic and digest bound | contract and configuration suites |
+| Native supervisor | `ginux-init` | Contract and route policy precede host effect; copied caller state is not trusted twice | native supervision and security suites |
+| Process authority | `ginux-process` | Virtual identity and descriptor generations cannot revive stale authority | process and multi-instance suites |
+| Virtual kernel views | `ginux-kernel-views` | Instance-visible state is generated from private Ginux state, not host trees | kernel-view and systemd suites |
+| Filesystem mediation | `ginux-fs-policy` after Gate 31 | Text and numeric descriptors are not authority; object identity and generations are required | Gate 31 host-effect oracles |
+| Package lifecycle | RPM spec, tmpfiles, and helper | Code is immutable package content; persistent and transient state have explicit ownership and lifecycle | RPM install, verify, erase, reinstall, and persistence tests |
+| Allocator | `ginux-allocator` after Gates 38-42 | Caller memory never contains allocator metadata; invalid ownership fails closed | allocator conformance, property, and Linux integration suites |
+
+### Installed trusted executables
+
+| Installed component | Privilege and host effects | Owner and invariant |
+|---|---|---|
+| `/usr/bin/ginux` | Operator process; requests lifecycle changes through `sudo` and the private helper protocol | `ginux-cli`; it validates public syntax but is not itself a privileged policy boundary |
+| `/usr/libexec/ginux/ginux-helper` | Root lifecycle, instance-root, console, deployment-state, and cache operations | Lifecycle helper; names are validated, paths remain below owned roots, and stopped/running transitions are checked before mutation |
+| `/usr/libexec/ginux/ginux-image` | Registry I/O, digest verification, blob caching, and root expansion | `ginux-oci`; verified content is committed atomically and extraction cannot escape its selected root |
+| `/usr/libexec/ginux/ginux-runtime` | `chroot`, identity reduction, ptrace supervision, virtual-kernel generation, syscall mediation, and workload execution | `ginux-init`; the immutable effective contract and virtual object identity govern every managed effect |
+| `/usr/lib64/ginux/libginux_allocator.so` | Optional workload-side virtual-memory calls and instance-private cgroup reads | `ginux-allocator`; it has no helper protocol or privileged path and remains subject to the workload contract |
+
+Test-only fixtures and benchmark binaries are not installed and are outside the
+runtime trusted computing base. The RPM file list is the authoritative package
+boundary; the inventory checker rejects drift in these installed paths.
+
+### Host-effect route matrix
+
+| Route | Authority snapshot | Decision point | Effect owner |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Instance create/import/set/edit | Complete candidate recipe plus deployment state | CLI validation, runtime normalization, then helper transition validation | Privileged lifecycle helper using atomic file replacement |
+| OCI fetch and expansion | Digest-pinned manifest, layer descriptors, and selected destination root | Descriptor validation and content digest verification before commit | `ginux-image` cache and extraction code |
+| Workload syscall | Entry registers plus one supervisor-owned copy of pointer-derived authority inputs | Immutable contract, operation catalog, dispatch lane, and object-generation checks | Native supervisor or an explicitly direct-safe host syscall |
+| Descriptor and virtual-object operation | Instance-local descriptor/object identity and generation | Contract decision plus type-specific owner lookup | Process, device, network, filesystem, or virtual-kernel owner |
+| Lifecycle stop/failure/recovery | Persisted instance identity plus verified live PID ownership | Helper state transition and stale-PID rejection | Helper cleanup of only that instance's transient resources |
+| Allocator memory operation | Checked size/alignment and instance-visible memory limit | Allocator ownership bitmap and checked reservation | Ordinary workload `mmap`, `mprotect`, `madvise`, and `munmap`, still mediated by Ginux |
+
+No managed route treats a text path, numeric PID, or descriptor number alone as
+authority. Direct-safe syscalls are limited to cataloged fixed semantics whose
+arguments do not require synchronous inspection, translation, virtual results,
+or a denial-side host effect.
+
+### Instance authority and revocation matrix
+
+| Authority class | Instance-private identity | Revocation evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| Processes and signals | Virtual PID plus generation mapped to one supervised host task | Process, native-process, multi-instance, and forced-teardown tests |
+| Descriptors and devices | Per-process descriptor entry plus backing-object generation | Process/device suites, descriptor reuse tests, and restart tests |
+| Root and writable storage | Validated instance directory and private expanded root | Multi-instance overlay checks and helper cleanup/reinstall tests |
+| Contract and audit | Normalized recipe digest, catalog version, deployment lock state, and instance-owned log | Contract mutation, lock/unlock, multi-instance, and package persistence tests |
+| Console | Instance-owned FIFO/log and verified supervisor identity | Attach/detach, signal, stale-state, and concurrent-console tests |
+| Network | Instance deployment attachment, forward ownership, and tracked socket identity | Network policy, bind-conflict, destination-snapshot, and multi-instance tests |
+| Virtual `/proc`, `/sys`, and cgroup | On-demand view derived from only the instance process/configuration state | Kernel-view, systemd, and distinct-memory-limit multi-instance tests |
+
+Numeric identity reuse is never sufficient to recover an old capability. Stop,
+failure, and restart discard transient generation-bearing state; persistent
+recipe and root state survive only where the documented lifecycle says they do.
+
+## Unsafe-code boundary
+
+Production Rust `unsafe` is confined to the freestanding native runtime and
+its syscall backends. Policy, configuration, virtual process, device, kernel
+view, VFS, OCI, and public CLI libraries remain safe Rust. The runtime requires
+raw startup pointers, register access, inline host syscalls, ptrace memory
+transfer, and fixed supervisor-owned buffers; these are reviewed as trusted
+seams rather than described as automatically safe because they are written in
+Rust.
+
+`scripts/check-security-inventory.sh` rejects new production unsafe syntax
+outside the approved runtime files. It also checks the retained privilege and
+package boundary markers. This is a structural tripwire, not a proof that each
+unsafe expression is correct.
+
+The approved unsafe/FFI/assembly owners are:
+
+| Source | Reason it is trusted |
+|---|---|
+| `ginux-init/src/main.rs`, `launch.rs`, `native_clone.rs`, `native_network.rs`, and `supervisor.rs` | Freestanding entry, raw Linux syscalls, ptrace register and tracee-memory transfer, and fixed-buffer supervision |
+| `ginux-allocator/src/lib.rs` | C ABI exports, raw allocation pointers, atomics, and Linux virtual-memory calls |
+| `baseline-runner` and `process-native-fixture` | Test/benchmark-only raw syscall fixtures; not installed runtime components |
+
+The x86-64 startup assembly is owned by `ginux-init/src/main.rs`; no separate
+production assembly object is installed. All other production policy and state
+libraries are required to remain free of Rust `unsafe` syntax by the inventory
+tripwire.
+
+## Dispatch and host effects
+
+The immutable effective contract is authoritative. Selective dispatch may
+continue only an explicitly allowed operation whose pinned catalog semantics
+say it needs no synchronous inspection, translation, virtual result, or audit.
+Paranoid dispatch has no direct path. A denial or complex operation is decided
+by Ginux before the requested host syscall. Mutable caller structures that
+affect authority must be copied once into supervisor-owned state.
+
+Gate 31 separately owns the filesystem call-context and capability foundation.
+Its result is referenced here and is not generalized to operation families that
+have not passed their own host-effect oracles.
+
+## Host privilege and installed state
+
+Root-launched workloads set `PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS`, drop to host UID/GID 65534,
+and retain only Ginux virtual credentials. The helper remains privileged and is
+part of the trusted computing base. Persistent instance and OCI state lives
+under `/var/lib/ginux`; transient control state lives under `/run/ginux`.
+Package verification and state-preserving reinstall tests are mandatory.
+
+| State | Owner/mode boundary | Lifecycle |
+|---|---|---|
+| `/etc/ginux/examples` | RPM-owned, read-only example recipes | Replaced only by package lifecycle rules |
+| `/var/lib/ginux/cache/oci` | Root-owned verified content-addressed cache | Preserved across package erase; corrupt content is rejected and may be reacquired |
+| `/var/lib/ginux/instances/NAME` | Root-owned recipe, deployment state, private root, console, and audit data | Created/replaced atomically; retained across package reinstall; explicit instance removal owns deletion |
+| `/run/ginux/instances/NAME` | Root-owned transient control state | Recreated at boot/start and revoked on stop or recovery |
+
+The helper accepts only its versioned private protocol. Operator environment
+values are not promoted into privileged paths without command-specific parsing;
+workloads do not inherit helper descriptors, ambient capabilities, or root host
+identity.
+
+OCI manifests and layers are verified before use. Cache corruption fails
+closed. Catalog changes do not silently recompile persisted wildcard contracts,
+because doing so could change their meaning.
+
+## Residual limitations
+
+- The balanced backend trusts the host kernel's ELF loading, virtual memory,
+  scheduling, signal, ptrace, and page-fault behavior.
+- Ginux is not a hardware isolation boundary and is not VM-equivalent.
+- The native runtime has a large manually reviewed unsafe surface. Structural
+  inventory, fuzzing, and regression tests reduce risk but do not constitute a
+  complete formal proof.
+- Shared-host networking intentionally shares normal host routing and bind
+  conflicts. Ginux mediates configured operations but does not claim a private
+  network namespace.
+- Formal assurance is component-specific. Proof or model-checking evidence
+  applies only to the named functions, state transitions, and assumptions.
+- No review can establish the absence of every defect. Security claims remain
+  limited to the documented invariants and passing evidence.
+
+## Review discipline
+
+Potentially sensitive findings are fixed before actionable detail is placed in
+public gate records. Every finding must end in a regression, an explicitly
+accepted limitation, or a separately approved follow-up gate. A clean review
+never replaces the complete build, package, integration, recovery, and
+multi-instance test matrix.
diff --git a/docs/security-boundary.md b/docs/security-boundary.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a5d3271
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/security-boundary.md
@@ -0,0 +1,146 @@
+# Security boundary and limitations
+
+Ginux is research software and is **not production-ready**. This document
+states what the current executable evidence supports, what remains trusted,
+and what has not been established. It is not an independent security review or
+a claim of universal exploit prevention.
+
+## Supported boundary claims
+
+- A managed syscall is resolved through the compiled instance contract and
+  selected dispatch mode before its permitted host effect. Unknown operations
+  fail closed.
+- Paranoid dispatch sends every syscall through the Ginux userspace decision
+  path. Selective dispatch permits direct execution only for operations that
+  are both contract-allowed and independently cataloged as transparent and
+  direct-safe. Denials and complex operations remain Ginux decisions.
+- Ginux supplies instance-owned process identities, descriptors, devices,
+  `/proc`, `/sys`, and cgroup views. Host kernel trees are not projected into
+  an instance.
+- The balanced native supervisor drops a root-origin host identity to UID/GID
+  65534, clears supplementary groups, has no effective capabilities, enables
+  `NoNewPrivs`, and closes inherited descriptors after acquiring the bootstrap
+  resources required by the selected dispatch mode.
+- Supervisor loss terminates traced workloads through `PTRACE_O_EXITKILL`.
+  Unexpected trace stops fail the instance closed and trigger cleanup.
+- Concurrent instances retain independent roots, writable state, contracts,
+  consoles, audit identity, virtual PIDs, synthetic kernel views, and lifecycle
+  state. Supported virtual process operations cannot address a peer instance's
+  host process identity.
+
+These claims apply only to the checked-in x86-64 Rocky Linux configurations,
+catalog, tests, and named execution modes. They are not a complete Linux ABI
+or distribution-compatibility guarantee.
+
+## Residual supervisor authority
+
+The balanced backend deliberately retains authority that it still needs:
+
+- ptrace control over its managed tasks;
+- one already-open descriptor for the assigned instance root;
+- standard input, output, and error for the selected console or log;
+- an optional controller channel used to forward console interrupts; and
+- in paranoid mode, a reduced child broker holding the seccomp notification
+  descriptor needed for `sched_yield` mediation.
+
+The notification and controller brokers inherit the reduced host identity and
+`NoNewPrivs`. They close the instance-root descriptor when they do not need it.
+The retained root descriptor and ptrace relationship remain security-critical
+Ginux authority; a defect in their validation or use can violate the intended
+boundary.
+
+### Filesystem mediation foundation
+
+The native filesystem lane currently claims no syscall. Before later gates can
+enable an operation family, both configured native routes now provide one
+immutable x86-64 call context containing all six syscall arguments, virtual and
+host task identity, instance lifetime and root generation, descriptor-table
+generation, typed root/cwd/descriptor authority, and the effective contract
+decision. A filesystem hook is eligible only when contract permission,
+route-local confinement, operation ownership, object identity, rights, and
+current generations all agree.
+
+The generated [filesystem operation inventory](fs-operation-inventory.md)
+assigns every currently identified filesystem-capable ABI entry to one owner or
+explicit denial. Unknown ABI entries, unknown operations, incomplete authority,
+stale generations, and contract denials fail closed. The retained root is
+identified from its already-open descriptor; path text cannot replace that
+identity. This foundation does not itself add filesystem compatibility or
+authorize any new host effect.
+
+## Trusted and excluded components
+
+The Host kernel and ptrace implementation, CPU and hardware, boot chain,
+approved Ginux binary and configuration, and operator remain trusted. The
+balanced backend also relies on the host kernel for ELF loading, address
+spaces, page faults, signals, and scheduling. A host-kernel vulnerability
+reachable through an allowed direct-safe syscall or those execution services
+is outside the boundary that Ginux currently implements.
+
+Hardware side channels, malicious firmware, physical attacks, a compromised
+host kernel, and operator or configuration compromise are excluded. Ginux has
+not received formal verification, a third-party penetration test, or an
+Independent security review.
+
+Ginux has no guest kernel or hardware-enforced guest boundary. Its current
+native backend is not VM- or microVM-equivalent isolation. See
+[How Ginux differs from UML, gVisor, namespaces, and VMs](how-ginux-differs.md)
+for the architectural comparison.
+
+## Known compatibility gaps
+
+Compatibility is intentionally contract-specific. Unsupported syscalls,
+ioctls, device operations, procfs/sysfs nodes, namespaces, and Linux behavior
+may return `EPERM`, `ENOSYS`, or another configured Linux error. The initial
+milestone covers one Rocky Linux 10 x86-64 systemd baseline and does not claim
+complete Linux conformance, arbitrary distributions, multiple architectures,
+physical-device passthrough, nested containers, private network namespaces, or
+production-scale multi-instance density. The bounded two-instance isolation
+evidence and shared-host networking limitation are documented in
+[multi-instance-isolation.md](multi-instance-isolation.md).
+
+## Performance limitation
+
+Gate 11's retained selective-mode matrix passes its CPU, memory, and mutex
+rows. Its thread-yield rows remain approximately 0.917x to 0.953x native under
+x86-64 QEMU TCG and therefore miss the unchanged 4% budget. Same-harness
+controls attribute the remaining gap to the host seccomp/task-work path in
+that environment. Gate 11 remains partial; Gate 13 must not be read as
+accepting or hiding that performance limitation. A fresh matched comparison
+against current `main` shows that Gate 13 itself adds no per-workload
+regression beyond the existing 4% accepted-Ginux budget.
+
+Gate 15 reports native, selective, and paranoid measurements side by side using
+paired native controls and checked-in templates. Native is not a Ginux mode and
+provides none of the instance boundary. Selective and paranoid results must be
+read with their different syscall-control semantics; faster execution is not
+evidence of equivalent enforcement. See [performance.md](performance.md) for
+the reproducible protocol and interpretation rules.
+
+## Reproducible evidence
+
+Run the following from a checkout with the pinned images already cached:
+
+```sh
+make check-security-doc
+make fuzz-security
+make test-security
+make test-recovery
+make test-multi-instance
+make demo-gate-13
+```
+
+`make fuzz-security` uses checked-in corpora, a fixed PRNG seed, a 4 KiB input
+limit, five seconds per target, a 60-second suite bound, and a 256 MiB process
+limit. A crash or timeout preserves its current input under
+`build/security-fuzz/`. `make test-security` exercises malformed contracts,
+selectors, ELF images, VFS indexes, kernel-view paths, device ranges, syscall
+dispatch, filesystem capability generations, deterministic path-replacement
+races, peer/host tripwires, audit accounting, host-effect denial, host-view
+isolation, bounded resources, teardown, and live supervisor authority.
+`make test-recovery` exercises configured startup failure, supervisor loss,
+forced-stop cleanup, audit bounds, and controlled systemd shutdown.
+
+Passing these tests is regression evidence for the stated configuration. It
+does not replace careful source review, independent adversarial testing,
+production hardening, or monitoring of the trusted host kernel.
